{"id":161705,"accession_number":"2000.69","share_license_status":"CC0","tombstone":"Cleansing Medicinal Herbs in the Stream on a Spring Day (春泉洗藥圖), 1703. Yu Zhiding (Chinese, 1647–c. 1716). Handscroll; ink and color on silk; painting only: 36.2 x 132.5 cm (14 1/4 x 52 3/16 in.); overall in heigh: 38.7 cm (15 1/4 in.). The Cleveland Museum of Art, Severance and Greta Millikin Purchase Fund, 2000.69","current_location":null,"title":"Cleansing Medicinal Herbs in the Stream on a Spring Day","title_in_original_language":"春泉洗藥圖","creation_date":"1703","creation_date_earliest":1703,"creation_date_latest":1703,"artists_tags":["male"],"culture":["China, Qing dynasty (1644-1911)"],"technique":"Handscroll; ink and color on silk","support_materials":[],"department":"Chinese Art","collection":"ASIAN - Handscroll","type":"Painting","measurements":"Painting only: 36.2 x 132.5 cm (14 1/4 x 52 3/16 in.); Overall in heigh: 38.7 cm (15 1/4 in.)","dimensions":{"painting only":{"height":0.362,"height_inch":14,"height_inch_fraction":0.25,"width":1.325,"width_inch":52,"width_inch_fraction":0.1875},"overall in heigh":{"height":0.387,"height_inch":15,"height_inch_fraction":0.25}},"state_of_the_work":null,"edition_of_the_work":null,"copyright":null,"inscriptions":[{"inscription":"Title by Chen Yixi.","inscription_translation":null,"inscription_remark":null,"sortorder":1},{"inscription":"姑射仙人冰雪膚，新詩傳唱滿通都。\n極知遲暮難為別，更展春泉洗藥圖。","inscription_translation":"Colophon by Wang Shizhen 王士禛 (1634–1711): \n\nThis Immortal from Mt. Gushe is marked by icy skin of perfection. \nHis new verses are being sung and praised throughout the capital. \nWe feel keenly that, at the approaching dusk, it was hard to part our ways. \nSo let us roll out the painting of Cleansing the Herbs [for all to see].","inscription_remark":null,"sortorder":2},{"inscription":"Colophon by Chen Tingjing 陳廷敬 (1639–1712). \n\nColophon by Wang Hongxu 王鴻緒 (1645–1723), dated 1707. \n\nColophon by Song Luo 宋犖 (1634–1714). \n\nColophon by Liu Yan 劉巗. \n\nColophon by Jing Xikui 涇西夔. \n\nColophon by Liu Yan 劉巗. \n\nColophon by Cao Yin 曹寅. \n\nColophon by Wang Shihong 汪士鋐. \n\nColophon by Wang Shidan 王式丹 (1645–1718), dated 1712. \n\nColophon by Shi Shenyi 史申義 (1661–1712), dated 1712. \n\nColophon by Yin Yuqing 殷餘慶, dated 1712.","inscription_translation":null,"inscription_remark":"Shi Shenyi, as owner of the scroll, solicited colophons from his elders and peers between 1703 and 1712. In that final year of his life, Shi himself wrote a set of verses, seemingly in response to the good will and optimism of the previous writers. In part, his colophon reads: \n\nWho would take pity on a lonely man long suffering from illness? \nWhy was I mistaken as an immortal with bones of jade?","sortorder":3},{"inscription":"Colophon by Chen Shixia 陳時夏, dated 1729.","inscription_translation":null,"inscription_remark":"The last colophon by Chen Shixia was likely requested by Shi Shenyi's sons years after his death.","sortorder":4}],"exhibitions":{"current":[{"id":293212,"title":"Greeting the Spring (Chinese art rotation, galleries 240a, 239, 241c)","description":"<i>Greeting the Spring (Chinese art rotation, galleries 240a, 239, 241c)</i>. The Cleveland Museum of Art (organizer) (February 11-August 13, 2017).","opening_date":"2017-02-11T05:00:00"},{"id":538196,"title":"Pacific Standard Time: Growing and Knowing in the Gardens of China","description":"<i>Pacific Standard Time: Growing and Knowing in the Gardens of China</i>. The Huntington Library, Art Collections and Botanical Gardens, San Marino, CA (organizer) (September 14, 2024-January 6, 2025) https://huntington.org/exhibition/duotiangong-growing-and-knowing-gardens-china.","opening_date":"2024-09-14T04:00:00"}],"legacy":[{"description":"<em>Main Asian Rotation (Gallery 122)</em>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (January 22-April 22, 2002).","opening_date":"2002-01-22T00:00:00"},{"description":"<em>Main Asian Rotation (Gallery 122)</em>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (July 23-October 27, 2003).","opening_date":"2003-07-23T00:00:00"}]},"provenance":[{"description":"Shi Shenyi 史申義 [1661–1712], Jiangdu 江都, Jiangsu province, China, by descent to his family","citations":[],"footnotes":null,"date":"1703–12","sortorder":1},{"description":"Shi family collection, Jiangdu, Jiangsu province, China,","citations":[],"footnotes":[],"date":"1712–late 1700s or early 1800s","sortorder":2},{"description":"Zhang Yin 張崟 [1761–1829], Zhenjiang, Jiangsu province, China","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Zhang Yin was a painter from Zhenjiang, a city fairly close to the Shi family's hometown of Jiangdu.</div>"],"date":"late 1700s–early 1800s","sortorder":3},{"description":"Zaobao 皁保 [d. 1882]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Zaobao was a Manchu dignitary. His high-profiled career began with his jinshi degree in 1845 and ended with his role heading the Ministry of Justice in 1876.</div>"],"date":null,"sortorder":4},{"description":"(A dealer in Liuli Chang 琉璃廠, Beijing, China, sold to Zhou Zhaoxiang)","citations":[],"footnotes":null,"date":"early 1900s?","sortorder":5},{"description":"Zhou Zhaoxiang 周肇祥 [1888–1954]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Zhou Zhaoxiang, a juren in the last Qing period, was also active in the government of the Republic of China. He was a scholar and poet in addition to excelling in landscape and flower-and-bird paintings. He was eager to educate painters in China and involved himself in cultural exchanges with Japan.</div>"],"date":"early 1900s–?","sortorder":6},{"description":"Fujii Yurinkan Museum, Kyoto, Japan","citations":[],"footnotes":null,"date":null,"sortorder":7},{"description":"(James J. Freeman, Kyoto, Japan, sold to the Cleveland Museum of Art)","citations":[],"footnotes":null,"date":"?–2000","sortorder":8},{"description":"The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH","citations":[],"footnotes":null,"date":"2000–","sortorder":9}],"find_spot":null,"related_works":[],"former_accession_numbers":[],"did_you_know":"In the pavilion are some of Shi Shenyi’s scholarly accoutrements: a stack of books, an antique <em>zun</em> 尊 (wine vessel), a brush holder, an ink stone, and a small chime.","early_education_description":null,"artlens_description":null,"description":"A famous portraitist, Yu Zhiding here depicts the likeness of Shi Shenyi 史申義 (1661–1712), an official and poet. Master Shi, a sickly man, was deeply interested in medicinal science. The opening section of the scroll features a boy washing a fungus in fresh spring water, a basket of medicinal herbs by his side. Other servants carry a fan, a bundle of scrolls, and a cup of tea or hot wine. Master Shi sits in a bamboo grove, surrounded by fields of herbs. Returning swallows, pink peach blossoms, and blooming magnolia indicate spring, whereas the fungus conveys wishes for long life. The spring theme may express the artist’s wish for his friend’s recovery.","external_resources":{"wikidata":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60478003"],"internet_archive":["https://archive.org/details/clevelandart-2000.69-cleansing-medicinal"]},"citations":[{"citation":"Suzuki, Kei 鈴木敬. <em>Chūgoku kaiga sōgō zuroku </em>[中國繪畫總合圖錄= Comprehensive Illustrated Catalog of Chinese Paintings], 第1卷. アメリカ·カナダ篇[= vol. 1 American and Canadian collections]. Tōkyō: Tōkyō Daigaku Shuppankai, 1982.","page_number":"#A22-149","url":null},{"citation":"Zhou, Zhaoxiang 周肇祥, Zhao Heng 趙珩, and Hai Bo 海波.<em> Liulichang za ji </em>琉璃厂杂记. Beijing Shi: Beijing Yan shan chu ban she, 1995.","page_number":"Mentioned: pp. 17–18","url":null},{"citation":"Cleveland Museum of Art, “Recent Acquisitions Press Release,” October 6, 2000, Cleveland Museum of Art Archives.","page_number":"Mentioned: p. 2","url":"https://archive.org/details/cmapr4354"},{"citation":"Chou, Ju-hsi. \"A Qing Dynasty Handscroll.\" <em>Cleveland Art: Cleveland Museum of Art Members Magazine</em> 41, no. 3 (March 2001): 8–9.","page_number":"Mentioned and Reproduced: pp. 8–9","url":"https://archive.org/details/CMAMM2001-03/page/n7/mode/2up"},{"citation":"Chou, Ju-hsi and Anita Chung. <em>Silent poetry: Chinese paintings from the collection of the Cleveland Museum of Art</em>. Cleveland, OH: The Cleveland Museum of Art, 2015.","page_number":"Mentioned and Reproduced: pp. 425–430","url":null},{"citation":"Scheier-Dolberg, Joseph. “A Portrait of Ambition: Yu Zhiding’s Thatched Cottage at the Western Stream / 志氣寫真：以禹之鼎的《西溪草堂圖》為例的個案研究.” <em>Arts Asiatiques</em> 72 (2017): 59–80.","page_number":"Comparative Material.","url":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/44656689"},{"citation":"Scheier-Dolberg, Joseph. <em>Yu Zhiding and the Envisioning of the Early Qing World</em>. PhD dissertation, Columbia University, 2018.","page_number":"Comparative Material.","url":""},{"citation":"Campbell, Duncan M., Bo Zheng, Kathleen M. Ryor, Carla Nappi, Georges Metailie, Shanshan Liu, Huang Xiao, and Sarah Chen Huang. <em>Growing and Knowing in the Gardens of China</em>. Edited by Michelle Bailey, Nicholas K. Menzies, and Phillip E. Bloom. San Marino: The Huntington Library, Art Museum, and Botanical Gardens, 2024.","page_number":"Mentioned and Reproduced: cat. no. 17, pp. 150–157","url":""}],"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/2000.69","images":{"annotation":null,"web":{"url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2000.69/2000.69_web.jpg","width":"1263","height":"336","filesize":"381274","filename":"2000.69_web.jpg"},"print":{"url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2000.69/2000.69_print.jpg","width":"3400","height":"904","filesize":"2786822","filename":"2000.69_print.jpg"},"full":{"url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2000.69/2000.69_full.tif","width":"17076","height":"4542","filesize":"310264436","filename":"2000.69_full.tif"}},"alternate_images":[{"web":{"url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2000.69/2000.69_alt0_web.jpg","width":"1193","height":"893","filesize":"701497"},"print":{"url":"https://openaccess-cd